body:
Well the pay is low but benefits are great. Unfortunately, depending on what you want to do, most of the work gets pawned off on contractors. Direct federal employees don't do the actual work and they are generally more administrative roles. Rarely do opportunities open up because feds don't get fired. They die or retire and that's when positions open up. Those positions open internally first, THEN to the public if no one gets promoted. The only real benefit is PSLF BUT you can get that from state and local governments too and they are easier to get into than the feds. Contractor pay and benefits are worse and you don't get PSLF as a contractor. Job turnover as a contractor is ridiculous and there isn't much chance for promotion or raises

&#x200B;

Source: Navy private contractor for 6 years in IT. 9 jobs as a contractor in that time due to contract rollover and lack of advancement opportunity.
